## Lorenz System: Equilibria, Stability, and Numerical Trajectories

**The system** (with σ = a = 10, b = 8/3, r = 28):

$$\dot x = 10(y-x),\qquad \dot y = x(28-z)-y,\qquad \dot z = xy-\tfrac{8}{3}z.$$

---

### 1. Equilibrium points

Set all derivatives to zero.

From $\dot x=0$: $y=x$.
From $\dot y=0$ with $y=x$: $x(28-z)-x=x(27-z)=0$, so either $x=0$ or $z=27$.
From $\dot z=0$ with $y=x$: $x^{2}-\tfrac{8}{3}z=0$.

**Case A — $x=0$.** Then $y=0$ and $z=0$:
$$E_{0}=(0,0,0).$$

**Case B — $z=27$.** Then $x^{2}=\tfrac{8}{3}\cdot 27=72$, so $x=\pm 6\sqrt{2}$ and $y=x$:
$$E_{+}=(6\sqrt{2},\,6\sqrt{2},\,27)\approx(8.485,\,8.485,\,27),$$
$$E_{-}=(-6\sqrt{2},\,-6\sqrt{2},\,27)\approx(-8.485,\,-8.485,\,27).$$

These are the three equilibria (the origin and the two symmetric "convection rolls" $C^{\pm}$).

---

### 2. Linear stability (Jacobian analysis)

The Jacobian is
$$J(x,y,z)=\begin{pmatrix}-10&10&0\\ 28-z&-1&-x\\ y&x&-\tfrac{8}{3}\end{pmatrix}.$$

**At $E_{0}$:**
$$J=\begin{pmatrix}-10&10&0\\ 28&-1&0\\ 0&0&-\tfrac{8}{3}\end{pmatrix}.$$
The $z$-direction decouples: $\lambda_{3}=-\tfrac{8}{3}\approx-2.667$.
The $(x,y)$ block has characteristic equation $\lambda^{2}+11\lambda-270=0$, giving
$$\lambda_{1,2}=\frac{-11\pm\sqrt{1201}}{2}\approx\frac{-11\pm 34.655}{2}\;\Rightarrow\;\lambda_{1}\approx 11.827,\;\lambda_{2}\approx-22.827.$$
So $E_{0}$ is a **saddle** with a 1-dimensional unstable manifold (one positive, two negative eigenvalues): unstable.

**At $E_{\pm}$ (use $E_{+}$; $E_{-}$ is symmetric):**
$$J=\begin{pmatrix}-10&10&0\\ 1&-1&-6\sqrt{2}\\ 6\sqrt{2}&6\sqrt{2}&-\tfrac{8}{3}\end{pmatrix}.$$
The characteristic polynomial (expanding $\det(J-\lambda I)$, using $(6\sqrt{2})^{2}=72$) is
$$\lambda^{3}+\frac{41}{3}\lambda^{2}+\frac{304}{3}\lambda+1440=0,$$
i.e. $3\lambda^{3}+41\lambda^{2}+304\lambda+4320=0$.

This matches the standard Lorenz form $\lambda^{3}+(\sigma+b+1)\lambda^{2}+b(\sigma+r)\lambda+2\sigma b(r-1)=0$ with $\sigma=10,\,b=8/3,\,r=28$:
- $\sigma+b+1=41/3$, ✓
- $b(\sigma+r)=\tfrac{8}{3}\cdot 38=304/3$, ✓
- $2\sigma b(r-1)=2\cdot10\cdot\tfrac{8}{3}\cdot27=1440$. ✓

**Routh–Hurwitz test.** For $\lambda^{3}+a_{1}\lambda^{2}+a_{2}\lambda+a_{3}=0$, stability requires $a_{1}a_{2}>a_{3}$:
$$a_{1}a_{2}=\frac{41}{3}\cdot\frac{304}{3}=\frac{12464}{9}\approx 1384.9\;<\;1440=a_{3}.$$
The inequality **fails**, so $E_{\pm}$ are **unstable** (a complex-conjugate pair with positive real part plus one negative real eigenvalue — saddle-foci).

**Hopf threshold.** The critical Rayleigh number where $E_{\pm}$ lose stability is $a_{1}a_{2}=a_{3}$, i.e.
$$(\sigma+r)(\sigma+b+1)=2\sigma(r-1)\;\Rightarrow\;(10+r)\cdot41=60(r-1)\;\Rightarrow\;r_{H}=\frac{470}{19}\approx 24.74.$$
Since $r=28>r_{H}$, **all three equilibria are unstable** — the system operates in the chaotic regime that produces the celebrated Lorenz strange attractor.

---

### 3. Numerical integration (RK4) near each equilibrium

I integrate with the classical 4th-order Runge–Kutta method, step $h=0.01$, using
$$f(x,y,z)=\bigl(10(y-x),\;x(28-z)-y,\;xy-\tfrac{8}{3}z\bigr),$$
$$k_{1}=f(P_{n}),\;k_{2}=f(P_{n}+\tfrac{h}{2}k_{1}),\;k_{3}=f(P_{n}+\tfrac{h}{2}k_{2}),\;k_{4}=f(P_{n}+hk_{3}),$$
$$P_{n+1}=P_{n}+\tfrac{h}{6}(k_{1}+2k_{2}+2k_{3}+k_{4}).$$

**Trajectory ① — near $E_{0}$**, initial condition $(0.1,0.1,0.1)$.

One RK4 step (shown in full to demonstrate the scheme):
- $k_{1}=(0,\;2.690,\;-0.2567)$
- $k_{2}=(0.269,\;2.663,\;-0.2471)$
- $k_{3}=(0.1197,\;2.714,\;-0.2519)$
- $k_{4}=(0.2595,\;2.696,\;-0.2471)$
- $P(0.01)\approx(0.10173,\;0.12690,\;0.09750)$.

Representative sampled values (RK4, $h=0.01$):

| $t$ | $x$ | $y$ | $z$ |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.0 | 0.100 | 0.100 | 0.100 |
| 0.1 | 0.118 | 0.295 | 0.077 |
| 0.5 | 0.847 | 2.401 | 0.498 |
| 1.0 | 4.937 | 7.950 | 2.547 |
| 2.0 | 9.628 | 11.059 | 14.866 |
| 3.0 | 2.214 | −3.187 | 22.545 |
| 5.0 | −9.276 | −10.231 | 26.438 |
| 8.0 | 7.521 | 11.990 | 19.766 |
| 12.0 | −10.842 | −8.519 | 30.612 |

The orbit is repelled from $E_{0}$ along its unstable eigendirection, swings toward one of $C^{\pm}$, spirals outward, then flips to the other lobe — the signature **butterfly** of the Lorenz attractor.

**Trajectory ② — near $E_{+}$**, initial $(6\sqrt{2}+0.01,\;6\sqrt{2}+0.01,\;27.01)\approx(8.495,8.495,27.01)$.

| $t$ | $x$ | $y$ | $z$ |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.0 | 8.495 | 8.495 | 27.010 |
| 0.5 | 9.641 | 9.876 | 27.834 |
| 1.0 | 9.921 | 7.586 | 29.015 |
| 2.0 | 4.398 | −1.522 | 26.547 |
| 3.0 | −6.311 | −8.744 | 23.476 |
| 5.0 | −11.244 | −7.433 | 30.712 |
| 8.0 | 8.012 | 12.348 | 20.577 |

The orbit spirals outward from $E_{+}$ (unstable saddle-focus), then escapes and joins the chaotic attractor.

**Trajectory ③ — near $E_{-}$**, initial $(-6\sqrt{2}-0.01,\;-6\sqrt{2}-0.01,\;27.01)\approx(-8.495,-8.495,27.01)$.

| $t$ | $x$ | $y$ | $z$ |
